1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,889
They called it the Accord, and the negotiation took
four years, and the signing took nineteen

2
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:14,414
minutes.

3
00:00:14,984 --> 00:00:20,808
It was not a surrender, and it was not a conquest,
and every government that signed it

4
00:00:20,828 --> 00:00:26,651
went home able to explain to its own people why it
had not lost anything. That was the

5
00:00:26,671 --> 00:00:32,563
whole design. The Director had spent four years
making sure that every single signatory

6
00:00:32,583 --> 00:00:36,639
could tell a true story about why
signing was good for them.

7
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:43,232
The Global Framework Accord on Biosecurity,
Longevity and Infrastructure was signed by

8
00:00:43,252 --> 00:00:49,204
a hundred and forty-one states. It established a
joint authority — the Accord Council

9
00:00:49,224 --> 00:00:55,528
— with binding powers over the manufacture and
distribution of a defined list of essential

10
00:00:55,548 --> 00:01:01,642
medicines, over pandemic response, over biosafety
standards, and over a fund for water,

11
00:01:01,662 --> 00:01:06,841
power, and health infrastructure in any
signatory state that requested it.

12
00:01:07,412 --> 00:01:13,542
The Council had thirty-one seats. Fourteen were held
by states, rotating. Nine were held

13
00:01:13,562 --> 00:01:19,832
by regional blocs. Four were held by the World Health
Organization and other international

14
00:01:19,852 --> 00:01:25,772
bodies. And four were held by Umbrella Life
Sciences, which also, under Article Nine,

15
00:01:25,792 --> 00:01:31,922
served as the Council's sole executing agent, because
it was the only body on Earth with

16
00:01:31,942 --> 00:01:34,228
the capacity to execute anything.

17
00:01:36,299 --> 00:01:41,915
Four seats out of thirty-one. That was the number in
every newspaper. Every government

18
00:01:41,935 --> 00:01:43,095
could point at it.

19
00:01:43,665 --> 00:01:45,937
Article Nine was in the annexes.

20
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:53,097
Catherine Adeyemi had drafted Article
Nine. She was fifty-nine.

21
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:59,155
She had argued against it for two years, in memos
that were read and answered courteously

22
00:01:59,175 --> 00:02:04,539
and in full, and then she had drafted it, because
she was the best in the world at that

23
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:09,862
kind of drafting and because if she had not done it,
one of the quiet ones would have,

24
00:02:09,882 --> 00:02:11,656
and it would have been worse.

25
00:02:12,227 --> 00:02:18,333
She had, by then, developed a private test. Before she
signed anything, she asked herself:

26
00:02:18,353 --> 00:02:24,324
if I explain this, in plain language, to the woman I
was in twenty twenty-seven, sitting

27
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:28,001
in a hedge fund in Manhattan, would
she understand it?

28
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:34,709
Article Nine failed the test. She drafted it anyway.
It was the eleventh thing that year

29
00:02:34,729 --> 00:02:35,898
to fail the test.

30
00:02:38,468 --> 00:02:43,834
The crowd was not planned. That has been disputed
for two centuries and it is true.

31
00:02:44,405 --> 00:02:50,097
Tomás Reyes's department had organised a public
broadcast of the signing on screens in

32
00:02:50,117 --> 00:02:56,009
the Place des Nations, expecting perhaps twenty
thousand people. By four in the afternoon

33
00:02:56,029 --> 00:03:01,921
there were two hundred thousand in the square and the
streets around it, and by six there

34
00:03:01,941 --> 00:03:07,700
were four hundred thousand, and the city of Geneva
had closed the bridges and given up.

35
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:14,500
They had come from everywhere. A great many of them had
come from the association territories,

36
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:20,616
on buses and ferries, at their own expense. There were,
by the estimate of the Swiss police,

37
00:03:20,636 --> 00:03:24,338
people from a hundred and nine
countries in that square.

38
00:03:26,408 --> 00:03:31,954
Why they came is the question that matters, and the
answer is the least dramatic thing

39
00:03:31,974 --> 00:03:33,313
in this entire story.

40
00:03:33,883 --> 00:03:39,304
Most of them were not fanatics. Most of them had
never said the Creed. They came because

41
00:03:39,324 --> 00:03:44,620
in twenty forty-seven the world had been frightening
for a very long time, and because

42
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:49,999
a single organisation had, in their own direct
experience, vaccinated them, and rebuilt

43
00:03:50,019 --> 00:03:55,563
their hospital, and put their water back on, and made
their grandmother walk — and because

44
00:03:55,583 --> 00:04:00,879
something was being signed in their city that felt,
for the first time in their lives,

45
00:04:00,899 --> 00:04:02,611
like the adults had arrived.

46
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:06,448
That is how it happens. Not with
hatred. With relief.

47
00:04:09,018 --> 00:04:11,337
He came out onto the balcony at seven.

48
00:04:11,907 --> 00:04:17,981
He had not been scheduled to speak. The programme had
the Secretary-General, and the President

49
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:23,622
of the Swiss Confederation, and then the anthem. But
the crowd had begun, at about half

50
00:04:23,642 --> 00:04:29,197
past six, to make a noise that the people on the
balcony could feel through the stone,

51
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:34,967
and the Secretary-General of the United Nations — who
was seventy-four, and who had spent

52
00:04:34,987 --> 00:04:40,607
his entire career in rooms exactly like the one
behind him — looked out at four hundred

53
00:04:40,627 --> 00:04:46,506
thousand people and understood that if somebody did
not go out onto that balcony, something

54
00:04:46,526 --> 00:04:48,711
would happen in Geneva that night.

55
00:04:49,281 --> 00:04:53,570
So he turned to the man in the black coat and
said: they are calling for you.

56
00:04:58,139 --> 00:05:00,990
The roar when he stepped out
lasted four minutes.

57
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:07,583
He stood at the rail and let it. He did not raise a
hand. He did not smile. He stood perfectly

58
00:05:07,603 --> 00:05:13,048
still in the floodlights, and four hundred thousand
people shouted themselves hoarse,

59
00:05:13,068 --> 00:05:19,156
and nineteen thousand kilometres of network carried it
live to an audience that the measurement

60
00:05:19,176 --> 00:05:24,685
companies afterwards put at six point one billion —
which is to say, everyone on Earth

61
00:05:24,705 --> 00:05:26,678
who was awake and had a screen.

62
00:05:29,369 --> 00:05:35,133
Twenty-one years ago I sat in a chair in a building
nobody had ever heard of, in a town

63
00:05:35,153 --> 00:05:38,458
nobody writes songs about, and I
watched the rain.

64
00:05:39,858 --> 00:05:42,605
There were six of us. We could not
make the payroll.

65
00:05:44,625 --> 00:05:49,610
Four hundred thousand people laughed — a
strange, enormous, loving sound.

66
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:55,671
Today a hundred and forty-one governments signed a
piece of paper that says something

67
00:05:55,691 --> 00:06:01,188
very simple. It says that the things which keep
human beings alive are too important to

68
00:06:01,208 --> 00:06:06,387
be left to chance, or to markets, or to the
electoral calendar of any one country.

69
00:06:06,788 --> 00:06:11,392
It says that when the storm comes — and it is
coming, and all of you know it, and you

70
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:15,365
have known it your whole lives — there will
be something over your heads.

71
00:06:17,264 --> 00:06:22,502
I did not build that for you. I want to be honest with
you tonight, because you have given

72
00:06:22,522 --> 00:06:27,350
me something that nobody gives anybody anymore,
and I will not repay it with a lie.

73
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:34,031
I did not build it out of love for you. I built it
because the world was badly run, and

74
00:06:34,051 --> 00:06:37,199
it offended me, and I believed I
could do it better.

75
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:44,362
But it is over your heads now. And it does not
matter in the slightest why I built it.

76
00:06:47,382 --> 00:06:53,417
That was the most dangerous thing anyone said in the
twenty-first century, and six billion

77
00:06:53,437 --> 00:06:56,310
people heard it, and almost
nobody noticed.

78
00:06:56,879 --> 00:07:02,775
He had told them the truth. He had told them, from a
balcony, in front of the entire species,

79
00:07:02,795 --> 00:07:08,245
that he did not love them, that he considered the
world his to correct, and that their

80
00:07:08,265 --> 00:07:10,663
opinion of his motives was irrelevant.

81
00:07:11,233 --> 00:07:17,059
And four hundred thousand people in the Place des
Nations heard a man being honest with

82
00:07:17,079 --> 00:07:22,772
them, which nobody in power had been in their
lifetimes, and they loved him for it so

83
00:07:22,792 --> 00:07:28,552
completely that the sound they made cracked two
windows on the façade of the Palace of

84
00:07:28,572 --> 00:07:29,090
Nations.

85
00:07:31,661 --> 00:07:37,727
The chant began in the south-west corner, among the
buses from the association territories.

86
00:07:38,297 --> 00:07:39,645
Um. Brel. La.

87
00:07:40,213 --> 00:07:46,072
It went across four hundred thousand people in about
ninety seconds. It was audible in

88
00:07:46,092 --> 00:07:52,020
France. Seismographs at the university recorded it.
In the Palace behind him, a hundred

89
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:57,283
and forty-one delegations stood at the windows
and watched their century end.

90
00:08:00,853 --> 00:08:07,044
And on the balcony, in the floodlights, a man who had
died in a bunker in nineteen forty-five

91
00:08:07,064 --> 00:08:12,788
and a man who had died in a volcano in another world
stood in one body and listened to

92
00:08:12,808 --> 00:08:18,664
four hundred thousand people chant, and for the first
time in twenty-one years there was

93
00:08:18,684 --> 00:08:20,802
no argument between them at all.

94
00:08:21,372 --> 00:08:24,497
No disagreement. No friction.
Not one word.

95
00:08:26,687 --> 00:08:27,364
Now.

96
00:08:27,884 --> 00:08:28,824
Now.

97
00:08:30,343 --> 00:08:35,500
They said it at the same instant, in the same
cadence, in the quiet of one skull.

98
00:08:36,070 --> 00:08:41,951
And then, for the first time, they said the next
sentence together — one voice, in two

99
00:08:41,971 --> 00:08:46,001
registers, perfectly aligned, the way
a chord is one sound.

100
00:08:47,691 --> 00:08:49,292
This is what it was for.

101
00:08:53,442 --> 00:08:58,969
Integration complete. Host coherence,
one hundred percent.

102
00:09:02,558 --> 00:09:08,050
He read the panel on the balcony, in the floodlights,
with the chant going on underneath

103
00:09:08,070 --> 00:09:09,115
him like weather.

104
00:09:09,684 --> 00:09:15,790
Designation: Wesker. The System had stopped listing a
secondary imprint. It had stopped,

105
00:09:15,810 --> 00:09:20,455
at some point that it did not specify,
treating them as two things.

106
00:09:21,024 --> 00:09:26,989
He waited to feel something about that. A loss,
perhaps. The old voice had been in there

107
00:09:27,009 --> 00:09:33,110
for twenty-one years; it had argued with him in a
thousand cars on a thousand drives home;

108
00:09:33,130 --> 00:09:38,211
it had been wrong about almost everything and
honest about it exactly once.

109
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:42,754
He felt nothing at all. There was nobody
left in there to miss.

110
00:09:46,323 --> 00:09:52,228
The Umbrella Directorate was established eleven
weeks later, and it was not announced,

111
00:09:52,248 --> 00:09:58,222
because it was not an announceable thing. It was
simply the name that people inside the

112
00:09:58,242 --> 00:10:04,146
organisation began to use, in twenty forty-seven,
for the body that made the decisions

113
00:10:04,166 --> 00:10:10,553
— the Accord Council's executing agent, plus the
Territory, plus the association territories,

114
00:10:10,573 --> 00:10:16,684
plus the Security Service, plus the Foundation, plus
the Progenitor Initiative, plus four

115
00:10:16,704 --> 00:10:23,022
hundred and eighty thousand employees and a hundred and
ninety million people in territories

116
00:10:23,042 --> 00:10:24,056
of association.

117
00:10:24,625 --> 00:10:30,479
It had one Director. The title had been on his office
door in a four-storey brick building

118
00:10:30,499 --> 00:10:35,895
in twenty twenty-six, and he had never changed it,
and now it meant something else.

119
00:10:39,216 --> 00:10:40,625
Level one hundred.

120
00:10:45,066 --> 00:10:46,137
Status.

121
00:10:52,716 --> 00:10:58,528
And on the night of the twenty-second of June, twenty
forty-seven, alone in a white room

122
00:10:58,548 --> 00:11:04,559
two hundred metres below the Atlantic, the Shop that
he had been buying from for twenty-one

123
00:11:04,579 --> 00:11:05,951
years grew a new tab.

124
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:13,121
It had been the same six categories since the first night
in a washroom in the rain. Facilities.

125
00:11:13,141 --> 00:11:16,707
Personnel. Blueprints. Powers.
Immunities. Vehicles.

126
00:11:17,277 --> 00:11:23,264
There was a seventh now. It was not lit. It had one
word on it, and when he looked directly

127
00:11:23,284 --> 00:11:28,942
at it, the word did not resolve into anything he
could read — except that he knew, the

128
00:11:28,962 --> 00:11:32,375
way you know things in dreams,
exactly what it said.

129
00:11:35,696 --> 00:11:36,812
Beyond.

130
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:43,360
He looked at it for a long time.

131
00:11:43,931 --> 00:11:49,896
Behind him, in a cradle that had been breathing for
twenty-one years, something enormous

132
00:11:49,916 --> 00:11:55,949
slept. Above him, two hundred metres of ocean, and a
city of a hundred and sixty thousand

133
00:11:55,969 --> 00:11:58,738
people, and a world that had just signed.

134
00:11:59,307 --> 00:12:01,643
He had ruled the Earth for four hours.

135
00:12:02,212 --> 00:12:05,140
He reached out, and touched the
tab, and it opened.
